  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}+4x=12.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}+4x+4$ is a perfect square.*",
  "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x+4=(x+2{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2}-4 & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2{)}^{2}-4+4 & =12+4 \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2{)}^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+2{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x+2| & =4 \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-2-4=-6 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-6{)}^{2}+{2}^{2}=40.\n$$",
